
    Guenomu

Hierarchical Bayesian procedure to estimate the distribution of species trees based on multi-gene families data.

Installation: 
  - apt-get install libgtk2.0-dev libargtable2-dev
  - compilation is done with autotools. Example:
    $ mkdir build
    $ cd build
    $ $PATH_TO_PROG/configure
    $ make

  The programs will be on ./src and ./src/test. I think that the libgtk is not needed, but you may have problem with the
argtable library. If you are not an administrator (e.g. you are just a user on your cluster) you may have to install it
by hand on your local directory (e.g. /home/simpson/local) and then, when installing guenomu, you run configure as

    $ ../guenomu/configure PKG_CONFIG_PATH="/home/simpson/local/lib/pkgconfig/"

  The directory lib/pkgconfig is the one with information about where pkgconfig can find the libraries (files with .pc
termination).

This file is just a template, please refer to the wiki page for more up-to-date information.

_________________

Some featured bugs:
* program does not acept multifurcating trees -- only exception is an unrooted tree, which has the form (A,B,C); program
will not complain, it will FAIL if there is a polytomy. 

* the first tree in a collection inside a nexus file will determine if branch lengths are used or not. That is, if the
first tree doesn't have branch lengths, all other lengths (from other trees in file) will be ignored. Branch lengths are
not used however in current version. BTW the program assumes that a colon (":") indicates presence of branch lenghts, so
if you need you can add a dummy ":" to your first tree (and the program will input a value of 1. to missing lengths.) 

* all leaves from all gene trees must have a mapping to the list of species, the program does not accept gene names that
do not contain the name of species. E.g. leaves should be eco_1, or leleco if there is a species called eco -- the
program search for the exact pattern within gene names (from larger name to smaller, e.g. "leco" is searched for mapping before
"eco").

